{"source":"read_webpage","text":"arXiv:2403.11219v2 [cs.CL] 14 Mar 2024

Scaling Laws for Instruction Following in Small Language Models

Abstract. We study how instruction-following capability emerges in models below 7B parameters. Our central finding is that performance on held-out instruction sets scales smoothly with both parameter count and instruction diversity during fine-tuning. We fine-tune eight models ranging from 340M to 6.8B parameters on mixtures of synthetic and crowd-sourced instructions. Evaluation uses a rubric-based protocol where human annotators score responses on a five-point scale across adherence, completeness, and tone. Results indicate a power-law fit with R^2 = 0.93 across the scaling range. Notably, models trained exclusively on synthetic instructions plateau earlier, suggesting that natural distributional variance in human-written instructions provides a training signal not captured by template-generated data. We release our instruction mixtures and evaluation rubrics. Section 3 describes the data pipeline, Section 4 the training regimen, and Section 5 presents ablations on instruction length and linguistic markedness. Limitations include single-language evaluation (English only) and possible annotator bias in rubric scoring. Full experimental code is available at the URL listed in the repository section of the paper's project page."}

